    "translation": "The valiant Yudhāmanyu and the strong Uttamaujas, Abhimanyu the son of Subhadrā, and the sons of Draupadī — all of them indeed great charioteers.",
    "commentary": "Plain meaning, fully drawn out: Duryodhana closes his survey of the enemy with the younger generation, and the verse is built as two pairs crowned by a totalizing verdict. First pair — yudhāmanyuś ca vikrāntaḥ, Yudhāmanyu the valiant (vikrānta, striding forth, the pugnacious step of the single-combat champion), uttamaujāś ca vīryavān, Uttamaujas the strong (uttama-ojas, highest vigour, crowned again vīryavān, possessing vīrya): two Pāñcāla princes, brothers-in-arms of Dhṛṣṭadyumna, professionals of the duel rather than kings of territory — the enemy's cutting edge rather than its mass. Second pair — saubhadraḥ, the son of Subhadrā (Abhimanyu, Arjuna's boy by Kṛṣṇa's sister, the beloved nephew of the very counsellor Duryodhana lacks), draupadeyāś ca, and the sons of Draupadī (the five young Pāṇḍavas — Prativindhya, Sutasoma, Śrutakīrti, Śatānīka, Śrutasena — one fathered by each brother, the war's second generation standing in formation). Then the verdict, the heaviest concession of the whole muster: sarva eva mahārathāḥ — all, every single one (sarve, and eva to seal it: without exception), are mahārathas, great charioteers, commanders reckoned worth ten thousand men each, the rank Droṇa himself holds. Duryodhana, who opened at 1.3 by flattering Droṇa's teaching, ends by certifying that the teaching has been out-learned even by its pupils' sons. The catalogue-effect is cumulative dread — kingdom (1.4), kin-houses (1.5), and now children (1.6): past, present, and future all arrayed against him — yet each name tightens the second noose: every youth he lists was raised inside dharma's household, while his own camp's youth (Lakṣmaṇa and the Kaurava boys) go unmentioned, as if the future itself had already defected. Sarvatobhadra meaning, fully drawn out: the verse is nigada-vyākhyāta, needing no gloss, because its tātparya carries the whole doctrine's terror-point: karma-ghoratva in its most dreadful form — not the greybeards' mass but its self-renewal. Youth here figures navāṅkura, the fresh sprout-impulses of abhyāsa (practice): that even the 'sons' — derivative strengths, newly cultivated disciplines, practices one generation removed from their source — already rank as mahārathas proves that jñāna-karma samuccaya reproduces itself. Rightly yoked action does not age and thin; it generates heirs stronger than its origin, as Abhimanyu (the saubhadra, grace-born daring of the Subhadrā-line, auspiciousness itself made martial) out-dares his fathers, and the draupadeyas (five, one per Pāṇḍava, the five-fold fruit of Draupadī-fire — suffering transmuted — distributed across all five faculties) stand where their fathers stood. Vikrānta (the striding forth) is ākramaṇa-śakti, the power to advance into the field rather than tremble at its edge; uttama-ojas crowned vīryavān is ojas itself doubled — vitality plus valour, sap plus flame — Spanda-vigour at its youngest and therefore most terrible to the entrenched. Ekenaiva padena — sarva eva — Rāmakantha's unwritten gloss would seal it: totality belongs to the Self's side; delusion, which will boast 'many weapons, many skills' at 1.9–1.10, can count multitudes but never say 'all are great,' for what lacks unity cannot confer stature. Duryodhana's praise is thus unwilling testimony, extracted by fear, that the Self's army renews itself from within — the exact ground on which 2.12–2.13 (na tv evāhaṃ jātu nāsaṃ… dehino 'smin yathā dehe) will later teach that nothing real ever ceases to be renewed.",
