    "interpretiveNotes": [{"note": "Kashmir Series of Texts and Studies No. LXIV (1943): Bhagavadgita with Sarvatobhadra of Rajanaka Ramakantha, ed. Madhusudan Kaul Shastri. English commentary original to this project; mula not reproduced here."}],
    "translation": "Then — standing on the great chariot yoked with white horses — Mādhava and the son of Pāṇḍu blew their two divine conches.",
    "commentary": "Plain meaning, fully drawn out: the reply begins, and the verse is built as station then sounding. Tataḥ — 'then': the second tataḥ (after 1.13's) turns answer into antiphon — tumult (tumula) answered by order, mob-sound by music; Sañjaya's ear pivots shores, Kaurava din behind, Pāṇḍava diptych ahead. Śvetair hayair yukte mahati syandane sthitau — 'standing on the great chariot yoked with white horses': śvetaiḥ (white — purity, daylight, sattva made visible), hayaiḥ (horses, the rushing senses), yukte (yoked, disciplined — √yuj, the Gītā's own root: the senses not slain but harnessed), mahati (great — answering 1.3's mahatīm and 1.8's mahati with true greatness, magnitude of dharma rather than mass), syandane (in the flowing one, the chariot that glides — print sandane, same vehicle), sthitau (the two standing, dual — two figures, one stance: master and disciple upright together where Arjuna will later sink, 1.47). Mādhavaḥ pāṇḍavaś caiva — 'Mādhava and the Pāṇḍava, indeed': mādhava (descendant of Madhu — Kṛṣṇa by his gracious lineage-name, sweetness after the Madhu-slaying), pāṇḍava (the pale one's son — Arjuna by his father-line, whiteness answering the horses' whiteness), caiva (and indeed — the pair sealed); the verse names no offices (no Hṛṣīkeśa, no Dhanañjaya yet) — intimacy before majesty, persons before conches. Divyau śaṅkhau pradadhmatuḥ — 'they blew the two divine conches': divyau (dual — divine, heaven-born, against the bought brass of 1.13), śaṅkhau (dual — two shells, counted, against the uncounted mob), pradadhmatuḥ (dual imperfect of pra-√dham: they blew forth — the preverb pra- answering abhi- of 1.13's abhyahanyanta: forthright sounding vs. beaten-upon noise). Two breaths, one chariot, white team: the war's visual icon is set before its first named note (Pāñcajanya at 1.15). Sarvatobhadra meaning, fully drawn out: nigada-vyākhyāta — and the image is the śāstra's yantra in finished form. Śveta-hayas figure śuddha-sattva-vṛttis, the purified operations of mind (śveta = prakāśa, the clarity in which objects show truly); yukta names yoga itself — senses harnessed, not amputated (the Gītā never teaches indriya-nāśa, only indriya-vinigraha-yoga); mahat syandana is the deha-ratha of Kaṭha 1.3.3–4 (ātman charioteer-read anew: here Īśvara Himself holds the reins, grace driving nature); sthitau-dual figures the samuccaya-stance — jñāna (Mādhava, the Lord as puruṣa/īśvara, knower of the field) and karma (Pāṇḍava, the jīva as mumukṣu, worker in the field) standing as one Self on one vehicle before they sound as two voices. Tataḥ-tataḥ (1.13 → 1.14) is thus, inwardly, the moment recognition answers reverberation: tumula (fragmentation audible) does not fade — it is answered, drowned by structure. Divyau against bahavaḥ (1.9), nāmadheya-to-come against anāmaka-din, pṛthak Offshore-accountability (1.18's pṛthak pṛthak) prepared here in dual number: truth counts (two, named, divine), delusion merely multiplies (many, anonymous, bought). The chariot stands before it sounds — sthitau before pradadhmatuḥ — because posture precedes utterance in the śāstra: only the yoked can blow divinely, and only the standing pair can teach the seated faller (1.47) to rise (2.3's uttiṣṭha).",
    "keyPoints": ["tataḥ answers tataḥ: antiphon, order after tumult", "śvetair hayaiḥ: whiteness as visible sattva", "yukte: √yuj — harnessed senses, Gītā's root enacted", "mahati answers mahatīm/mahati: true greatness vs mass", "sthitau dual: two figures, one upright stance", "Mādhava + Pāṇḍava: intimacy before majesty", "divyau śaṅkhau: two counted divine vs uncounted mob", "pra- vs abhi-: forthright sounding vs beaten-upon noise", "deha-ratha with Īśvara driving: grace holds reins", "posture precedes utterance: standing pair will raise fallen one"]
